        "project_capabilities": {
            "catalogue": [
                {
                    "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/bitloop.md",
                    "id": "bitloop",
                    "summary": "# Bitloop project and skill routing\n\nActions: use the existing Bitloop lifecycle, CMake integration, drawing, camera/input and diagnostic facilities. Workers start with [bitloop-development](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/SKILL.md), then load only references relevant to their task. Planning/review receives this catalogue, not the linked manuals.\n\nPrerequisites: follow [Substrate instructions](../../AGENTS.md), identify the active target/build and inspect its Bitloop headers for exact APIs. Hints describe framework facilities, not implemented Substrate features or successful checks. Keep simulation ownership in this project; changes to framework/other owners require the applicable authorization.\n\nEvidence: compile the smallest relevant target for wiring; use semantic checks for physics, snapshots for visual claims and measured benchmarks for performance. Record capability IDs, actual commands/configuration, observations, limits and retained evidence paths in the MindGraph task result's supported fields."
                },
                {
                    "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/input.md",
                    "id": "input",
                    "summary": "# Scripted input and interaction checks\n\nActions: Bitloop's development headless runner accepts `--input FILE` with frame-scheduled client-space JSONL events/gestures: mouse movement/buttons/wheel, keyboard transitions, touch and drag gestures. A task can replay dragging an object from A to B and inspect before/after snapshots plus semantic position/ownership checks.\n\nPrerequisites: a compatible development build, the correct project/scene/viewport and client dimensions, implemented interaction handlers, deterministic setup and verified replay syntax in the active framework. Follow [input/events](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/input-events.md) and [camera/viewport](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/camera-viewport.md). Coordinates are client space, not world space. Event ownership, camera navigation and UI consumption affect the result.\n\nEvidence: retain the input JSONL, launch configuration, relevant frames, resulting state observations and inspected images. A replay file or an image path alone does not prove the interaction succeeded. Substrate interaction support must be checked per task."
                },
                {
                    "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/simulation-health.md",
                    "id": "simulation-health",
                    "summary": "# Behavioural horizons and temporary probes\n\nActions: combine focused numerical gates, cheap per-tick invariant monitors and adaptive complete-state inspections to investigate delayed, cumulative or rare failures. Route trajectory-changing work through [simulation health](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/simulation-health-diagnostics.md); use [temporary diagnostics](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/temporary-diagnostics.md) for short-lived probes.\n\nPrerequisites: project-defined physical invariants, meaningful scales, controlled configuration/seeds and a justified simulated horizon. Monitor logic and physical oracles must be implemented for Substrate; these workflows do not imply they already exist. Put disposable probes behind `BITLOOP_DEV_MODE`, outside production hot paths, and remove them when their purpose ends.\n\nEvidence: record dt/ticks/simulated time, timescales/event opportunities, monitor coverage, inspection schedule, worst-case observations, stopping rationale and remaining uncertainty. A short smoke run or clean final screenshot is insufficient for long-horizon claims. Preserve compact findings under `.codex/runs`; keep high-volume scratch in a manifest-owned session under the Codex build directory."
                },
                {
                    "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/snapshots-checkpoints.md",
                    "id": "snapshots-checkpoints",
                    "summary": "# Visual snapshots and explicit checkpoints\n\nActions: capture periodic layout images or targeted scene/viewport, pixel/world-region snapshots through Bitloop's capture pipeline. Save explicit simulation checkpoints and restore selected state for reruns when the project implements serialization. Follow [snapshots/checkpoints](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/checkpoints-snapshots.md).\n\nPrerequisites: a development build, rendering support and stable unambiguous scene/viewport selection for images; implemented save/load hooks and compatible configuration/scene IDs for checkpoints. Substrate's generated checkpoint hooks initially return false. Checkpoints are not automatic memory serialization and do not prove restart equivalence.\n\nEvidence: inspect actual images and correlate them with semantic traces. For restored runs, compare uninterrupted versus save/restore continuation using invariants, state fingerprints or justified tolerances. Record configuration, frame/time, image/checkpoint paths and observed outcomes. Use [visual QA](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/visual-qa.md) when appearance is part of acceptance; pixels alone cannot establish physics correctness."
                },
                {
                    "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/traces.md",
                    "id": "traces",
                    "summary": "# Headless semantic traces\n\nActions: run Bitloop development builds headlessly, list/select stable scene IDs, supply configuration overrides, advance a fixed timestep and record bounded semantic JSONL traces. Choose sparse or focused frame capture; reconstruct compact delta traces before analysing them. Reuse [tracing diagnostics](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/tracing-diagnostics.md).\n\nPrerequisites: `BITLOOP_DEV_MODE` tooling, a runnable target, supported scene configuration and implemented scene telemetry. Substrate's generated `traceFrame` hook initially has no semantic metrics; verify/implement the metrics required by the task. The framework does not supply this engine's physical oracle automatically.\n\nEvidence: retain command/configuration, seed, scene IDs, fixed dt, simulated horizon, trace paths and observed invariants/metrics under `.codex/runs/runNNNN_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS/`. Report numerical conclusions and their tolerances, not just successful exit or trace-file existence. Use focused tests for kernels and traces for temporal behaviour."
                }
            ],
